---
const entries = [
  {
    date: "2026-03-13",
    label: "Friday, March 13, 2026",
    audio: "/audio/briefing-2026-03-13.mp3",
    transcript: `Today is Friday, March 13, 2026 — a date so notoriously unlucky you might as well be walking under a ladder while simultaneously breaking a mirror and adopting a black cat. Let's dive right into the daily chaos, starting with the gentle yet foreboding tap on your shoulder known as reminders.

First up, you've scheduled the gym for 7am. Because nothing says "cruel start to the morning" like aggressively sweating before you've even had your coffee. Then, there's the task of calling your dentist to reschedule. Which, let's face it, is basically negotiating a peace treaty with someone determined to jab metal into your mouth. And, of course, don't forget your daily DSA practice — because solving two problems before breakfast is just how we roll in this dystopian future.

Now on to emails. Vercel says your deployment failed. Which is basically like receiving a message that your house caught fire while you were away on holiday. Stripe warns your trial ends in three days. In corporate speak: good luck not giving us money. And a heartwarming email from Mum — Dinner Sunday? Because it's never too early to start feeling guilty about your family obligations.

In AI news: OpenAI released GPT-5 with a million-token context window. It can digest your entire codebase and still have the memory of a sluggish goldfish. Google DeepMind claims their AI solves PhD-level math — until you realise it memorised every thesis in existence, rendering it the ultimate academic impersonator. And a startup raised $200 million to build AI that replaces AI startups. Investors called it the most efficient deployment of capital they have never understood.

So rally forth. Hit the gym, call your dentist, survive the emails, and maybe — just maybe — you will get through this Friday the 13th feeling slightly more in control. Good luck. Truly.`
  }
];
